ultramodern: deliver the hardware's messages at a yield point

A message from the video interface, the audio interface, the timer or the
signal processor is queued by a runtime thread and delivered by whichever
game thread next calls into the runtime. That is fine as long as one always
does. A game that waits by reading memory never does, and then the message
sits in the queue: the retrace never arrives, every other thread stays
blocked in `osRecvMesg`, and the console stops with nothing wrong anywhere
that a log would show.

`recomp_preempt` is what the recompiled code calls at a function entry when
this file has said there is something waiting. It does what the console's
interrupt does and in the same order: deliver everything the hardware has
been holding, then run whichever thread should be running now. Two things
stop it -- a thread that has turned interrupts off, and the game's own
exception handler, which the host marks while it is rewriting an overlay stub
and assembling a thunk. Both are cases where the console would not have
interrupted either.

--- a/ultramodern/src/scheduling.cpp
+++ b/ultramodern/src/scheduling.cpp
@@ -1,9 +1,54 @@
 #include "ultramodern/ultramodern.hpp"
 
+/// Set when a thread has been made runnable and nothing has looked since.
+///
+/// The recompiled code reads this at every function entry and calls
+/// `recomp_preempt` when it is set, which is the only way a thread that waits
+/// by reading memory rather than by receiving a message can ever be taken off
+/// the processor. See YIELD_POINT in recomp.h.
+extern "C" volatile unsigned recomp_preempt_wanted = 0;
+
+/// Non-zero while the thread is somewhere it must not be taken away from.
+///
+/// The console has one of these too: an exception handler runs with interrupts
+/// off. A game that reaches between its overlays through the syscall exception
+/// rewrites a stub and assembles a thunk in there, and a thread that saw half
+/// of that would jump into nothing.
+extern "C" thread_local unsigned recomp_no_preempt = 0;
+
+void dequeue_external_messages(RDRAM_ARG1);
+
+extern "C" void recomp_preempt(uint8_t *rdram, unsigned status_reg) {
+    // A thread that has turned interrupts off is in the middle of something it
+    // has said may not be interrupted. Leave the flag set and come back at the
+    // next entry after it turns them on again. A thread that has never written
+    // the status register has never said anything, and a zero there is that
+    // rather than a mask.
+    if (recomp_no_preempt != 0 || (status_reg != 0 && (status_reg & 1) == 0)) {
+        return;
+    }
+    recomp_preempt_wanted = 0;
+    // Only a thread the runtime knows about can be swapped away from. Anything
+    // else running recompiled code -- the boot path, before there are threads
+    // -- has nothing to hand over to.
+    if (ultramodern::this_thread() == NULLPTR) {
+        return;
+    }
+    // Everything the hardware has been waiting to deliver, and then whichever
+    // thread should be running now. This is the whole of what the console's
+    // interrupt does, and doing it here is what makes a loop that only reads
+    // memory into a loop something else can end.
+    dequeue_external_messages(PASS_RDRAM1);
+    ultramodern::check_running_queue(PASS_RDRAM1);
+}
+
 void ultramodern::schedule_running_thread(RDRAM_ARG PTR(OSThread) t_) {
     debug_printf("[Scheduling] Adding thread %d to the running queue\n", TO_PTR(OSThread, t_)->id);
     thread_queue_insert(PASS_RDRAM running_queue, t_);
     TO_PTR(OSThread, t_)->state = OSThreadState::QUEUED;
+    // Whoever is running now should look at the queue at its next chance, even
+    // if it is in a loop that never asks the runtime for anything.
+    recomp_preempt_wanted = 1;
 }
 
 void swap_to_thread(RDRAM_ARG OSThread *to) {
--- a/ultramodern/src/mesgqueue.cpp
+++ b/ultramodern/src/mesgqueue.cpp
@@ -27,12 +27,22 @@
     requeue_enabled.set(static_cast<int>(EventMessageSource::Dp), mqc.requeue_dp);
 }
 
+// A message from the hardware is what an interrupt is, and the running thread
+// has to be made to look at it. See YIELD_POINT in recomp.h: a game that waits
+// by reading memory rather than by receiving a message never asks the runtime
+// anything, so without this the message sits in the queue and the console
+// stops -- the retrace never arrives, the audio thread never runs, the sound
+// the loop is waiting for never finishes.
+extern "C" volatile unsigned recomp_preempt_wanted;
+
 void ultramodern::enqueue_external_message_src(PTR(OSMesgQueue) mq, OSMesg msg, bool jam, EventMessageSource src) {
     external_messages.enqueue({mq, msg, jam, requeue_enabled[static_cast<int>(src)]});
+    recomp_preempt_wanted = 1;
 }
 
 void ultramodern::enqueue_external_message(PTR(OSMesgQueue) mq, OSMesg msg, bool jam, bool requeue_if_blocked) {
     external_messages.enqueue({mq, msg, jam, requeue_if_blocked});
+    recomp_preempt_wanted = 1;
 }
 
 bool do_send(RDRAM_ARG PTR(OSMesgQueue) mq_, OSMesg msg, bool jam, bool block);
